#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a5e107b-3f50-5bd8-bbcd-305062ce3cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321099} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f67e50-fb3e-5c99-b805-8da7432be84a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321100} {:answer/id #uuid "41a4562c-39f9-59ee-9f23-b100e444c6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321101} {:answer/id #uuid "f2815d82-add3-5b62-b7a6-1f96bccbae5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321102} {:answer/id #uuid "42764dc4-d6a8-54de-ab9c-6e12289fb360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2815d82-add3-5b62-b7a6-1f96bccbae5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321102} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea54a922-cd8d-5604-8b3d-e3c059805f1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed7bcfe5-c1ea-56d9-b1e4-351fef6e9306" :question/math-academy-id "q-72005" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
